{"parentUuid":"74b1ba52-b967-4fbc-83e3-d4d814bda737","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a0ac44e2d03f4edb2","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019jkFQtrpKxENtqcukzVkY8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01E7AYbYdDKb9hdvLdmuJksf","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"\"solution hint\" CP-SAT warm start previous solution AddHint reoptimize"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8028,"cache_read_input_tokens":13121,"output_tokens":344,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8028},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":344,"cache_read_input_tokens":13121,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8028,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8028,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011Caox5HzAmbtV9a2APQzsz","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"48d09e58-b154-4c2e-9d20-c04ddccdf8c9","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:45.497Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"3605cf44-0d70-4fd3-bd02-d8718f43c464","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a0ac44e2d03f4edb2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014ouDTF27yR6e62kwDarrRV","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT OR-Tools relative_gap_limit progressive tightening incumbent hint warm start\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-SAT, UNKNOWN status with complete solution hint · Issue #3750 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3750\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"Coding Patterns - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/06_coding_patterns.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"cpmpy.solvers.ortools — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/cp_optimizer/_modules/cpmpy/solvers/ortools.html\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Optimality Tolerance in CP-SAT/SCIP\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/Yra_AIJ3kkE\"}]\n\nI found relevant information about CP-SAT and OR-Tools related to your search terms. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Relative Gap Limit\n\nThe relative limit stops the solver when the objective value is within a specified ratio of the bound. This parameter is used to control solution quality and stopping criteria in CP-SAT.\n\n## Warm Start and Solution Hints\n\nWarm-starting is an optimization where the solver uses the previous solution as a starting point or \"hint\" for the next iteration, and this technique can significantly speed up the solving process, as the solver can often use the previous solution as a good starting point for repair, even if the previous solution becomes infeasible due to a newly added constraint.\n\nBecause repairing an infeasible hint can be computationally expensive, CP-SAT handles this process carefully, and you can instruct CP-SAT to attempt repairing the hint by setting solver.parameters.repair_hint = True.\n\n## Incumbent Solutions\n\nIn the context of progressive tightening, CP-SAT's portfolio solver components engage in information exchange, sharing progress when better solutions emerge or tighter bounds become available.\n\n## Implementation Example\n\nOR-Tools supports warmstarting the solver with a feasible solution, more specifically, it will branch that variable on that value first if possible, which is known as 'phase saving' in the SAT literature, but then extended to integer variables.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"95df2951-8958-41e9-b15e-db56198da48e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:50.121Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3605cf44-0d70-4fd3-bd02-d8718f43c464","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"74b1ba52-b967-4fbc-83e3-d4d814bda737","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a0ac44e2d03f4edb2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011LBxmHwShYcXnG297MUJj9","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"anytime algorithm epsilon optimal progressive deepening combinatorial optimization MIP\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Effective anytime algorithm for multiobjective combinatorial optimization problems - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0020025521002310\"},{\"title\":\"Effective anytime algorithm for multiobjective combinatorial optimization problems\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2403.08807v1\"},{\"title\":\"Effective anytime algorithm for multiobjective combinatorial ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.08807\"},{\"title\":\"[2403.08807] Effective anytime algorithm for multiobjective combinatorial optimization problems\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.08807\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Anytime tree search for combinatorial optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/346063021_Anytime_tree_search_for_combinatorial_optimization\"},{\"title\":\"Approximation algorithm - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Approximation_algorithm\"},{\"title\":\"Multi-objective optimization - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-objective_optimization\"},{\"title\":\"Combinatorial Optimization Problem - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/combinatorial-optimization-problem\"},{\"title\":\"Combinatorial optimization - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combinatorial_optimization\"},{\"title\":\"Improving ant colony optimization algorithm with epsilon greedy and Levy flight | Complex & Intelligent Systems | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40747-020-00138-3\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding anytime algorithms with epsilon-optimality and their applications to combinatorial optimization and MIP:\n\n## Anytime Algorithms in Combinatorial Optimization\n\nAnytime algorithms are characterized by two properties: (i) they can be terminated at any time and still return an answer, and (ii) the answers returned improve in some well-behaved manner. The algorithm is anytime in the sense that it is possible to interrupt its execution and take the non-dominated set, and it is guaranteed that this set is well-spread in the objective space.\n\n## Epsilon Indicator in Solution Quality Assessment\n\nThe additive epsilon indicator gives the minimum additive factor by which the approximation set has to be translated in the objective space in order to weakly dominate the reference set. This metric is commonly used to evaluate solution quality in anytime algorithms.\n\n## Multiobjective Combinatorial Optimization\n\nRecent research has developed exact anytime algorithms for multiobjective combinatorial optimization combining novel ideas to enhance the anytime behavior. These algorithms are evaluated using multiple performance measures including the overall non-dominated vector generation ratio, the hypervolume, the general spread and the additive epsilon indicator.\n\n## Tree Search and MIP Applications\n\nTree search algorithms are used in a large variety of applications (MIP, CP, SAT, metaheuristics with Ant Colony Optimization and GRASP) and also in AI/planning communities.\n\nThe research demonstrates that anytime algorithms can be effectively applied to MIP and other combinatorial optimization problems, providing progressively improving solutions with measurable epsilon-optimality guarantees.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"af5f66af-f7fd-48db-bddb-543fa2bbd49a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:51.761Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"74b1ba52-b967-4fbc-83e3-d4d814bda737","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"48d09e58-b154-4c2e-9d20-c04ddccdf8c9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a0ac44e2d03f4edb2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E7AYbYdDKb9hdvLdmuJksf","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"solution hint\" CP-SAT warm start previous solution AddHint reoptimize\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"Warm Start Optimization. Some introduction to the concept of… | by AlainChabrier | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@AlainChabrier/warm-start-optimization-ac73eef189e9\"},{\"title\":\"Coding Patterns - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/06_coding_patterns.html\"},{\"title\":\"cpmpy.solvers.ortools — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/cp_optimizer/_modules/cpmpy/solvers/ortools.html\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"✨ Recipes for Google OR-Tools | Just another developer\",\"url\":\"https://xiang.es/posts/cp-sat/\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding CP-SAT: Constraint Programming Meets SAT Solving | by Jonas Poelmans | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@jonaspoelmans120/understanding-cp-sat-constraint-programming-meets-sat-solving-b137dddb943c\"},{\"title\":\"warm start cp-sat ortools\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/minizinc/c/1QRwbQhStHg\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"}]\n\nI found several relevant results about CP-SAT warm starting with solution hints. Here's a summary:\n\n## CP-SAT Warm Starting with `AddHint`\n\nWarm-starting is an optimization where the solver uses the previous solution as a starting point or \"hint\" for the next iteration. This technique can significantly speed up the solving process, as the solver can often use the previous solution as a good starting point for repair.\n\n### How to Use `AddHint`\n\nOR-Tools supports warmstarting the solver with a feasible solution - more specifically, it will branch that variable on that value first if possible, which is known as 'phase saving' in the SAT literature, but then extended to integer variables.\n\nThe solution hint does NOT need to satisfy all constraints, it should just provide reasonable default values for the variables.\n\n### Repairing Hints\n\nWhen re-optimizing with modified constraints, because repairing an infeasible hint can be computationally expensive, CP-SAT handles this process carefully, and you can instruct CP-SAT to attempt repairing the hint by setting `solver.parameters.repair_hint = True`.\n\n### Best Practices\n\nWhen available, provide solution hints - for daily replanning where yesterday's solution is available, use it as a hint, and the solver doesn't blindly trust hints but uses them to focus initial search on promising regions. Additionally, CP-SAT prefers full hints, meaning all variables.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d8c2f69f-0175-4384-a5a8-16db374d30db","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:52.924Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"48d09e58-b154-4c2e-9d20-c04ddccdf8c9","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"efe15480-10c8-407f-930a-0ce395824462","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a0ac44e2d03f4edb2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013B15h4PV1HJvW9ZYUBJaQJ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Gurobi MIPGap progressive tightening anytime algorithm two phase solve incumbent\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"What is the MIPGap? – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/articles/8265539575953-What-is-the-MIPGap\"},{\"title\":\"Parameter Reference - Gurobi Optimizer Reference Manual\",\"url\":\"https://docs.gurobi.com/projects/optimizer/en/current/reference/parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"Optimization Algorithms FAQ: Simplex, Barrier, and MIP Methods | Gurobi\",\"url\":\"https://www.gurobi.com/resources/faq/optimization-algorithms-faq-simplex-barrier-and-mip-methods\"},{\"title\":\"Getting Stuck in gap in MIP optimization model – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/20856079255441-Getting-Stuck-in-gap-in-MIP-optimization-model\"},{\"title\":\"Parameter Guidelines - Gurobi Optimizer Reference Manual\",\"url\":\"https://docs.gurobi.com/projects/optimizer/en/current/concepts/parameters/guidelines.html\"},{\"title\":\"User MIP start did not produce a new incumbent solution/ Another try with MIP start – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/360073235912-User-MIP-start-did-not-produce-a-new-incumbent-solution-Another-try-with-MIP-start\"},{\"title\":\"Is it possible to change when (the gap %level) Gurobi starts focusing on finding feasible solutions instead finding a lower lower bound? – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/20206323134353-Is-it-possible-to-change-when-the-gap-level-Gurobi-starts-focusing-on-finding-feasible-solutions-instead-finding-a-lower-lower-bound\"},{\"title\":\"GUROBI Options — AMPL Resources\",\"url\":\"https://dev.ampl.com/solvers/gurobi/options.html\"},{\"title\":\"Taking Your Optimization Skills to the Next Level | Towards Data Science\",\"url\":\"https://towardsdatascience.com/taking-your-optimization-skills-to-the-next-level-de47a9c51167/\"},{\"title\":\"Multiple Objectives: MIPGap of each objective – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/360048036491-Multiple-Objectives-MIPGap-of-each-objective\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about Gurobi's MIPGap and related optimization strategies:\n\n## MIPGap and Gap Behavior\n\nThe gap improvement in Gurobi happens in jumps rather than continuous progression, with the gap computed using the formula: |ObjBound - ObjVal| / |ObjVal|.\n\n## Two-Phase Solution Strategy\n\nGurobi implements a strategy-switching mechanism relevant to your query: The MIP solver can change parameter settings in the middle of the search to adopt a strategy that gives up on moving the best bound and instead devotes all of its effort towards finding better feasible solutions, with a parameter to specify when this switch occurs. When optimize() is called multiple times, the solver will continue the same optimization run with updated parameters, enabling a two-phase approach where you can update the MIPGap parameter during solving.\n\n## Progressive MIPGap Tightening\n\nWhile the search results don't explicitly mention \"progressive tightening\" by that name, the solver continues the same optimization run with updated parameters in subsequent optimize() calls, which supports implementing a dynamic MIPGap strategy where you can tighten the gap requirement over time or based on progress.\n\n## Incumbent Solutions\n\nIf you stop a MIP solve early, you get the best feasible solution found (the incumbent) and a best bound, with the relative optimality gap summarizing the remaining uncertainty between those two values.\n\nThe search results suggest that Gurobi supports the kinds of dynamic solving strategies you're asking about, though a more specific technical paper or documentation would provide greater detail on the exact \"anytime algorithm\" implementation.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"30c29397-78f0-4730-b12e-5cd63c0d7e8c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:56.016Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"efe15480-10c8-407f-930a-0ce395824462","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
