{"parentUuid":"6e34d2c4-cad4-4720-89ed-4e6764b8e494","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a0ac44e2d03f4edb2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B73y3gZPgh6975LJrnHwgt","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"time limit\" \"MIP gap\" rolling horizon industrial scheduling production planning anytime\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Full article: A novel machine-learning rolling horizon heuristic for dynamic lot-sizing and job shop scheduling problems\",\"url\":\"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00207543.2025.2453651\"},{\"title\":\"Rolling horizon based planning and scheduling integration with production . . . | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228610800_Rolling_horizon_based_planning_and_scheduling_integration_with_production\"},{\"title\":\"Rolling horizon based planning and scheduling integration with production capacity consideration - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S000925091000477X\"},{\"title\":\"MIP-based heuristics for multi-item capacitated lot-sizing problem\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2005/11/1244.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"MIP Heuristics for a Resource Constrained Project Scheduling Problem with Workload Stability Constraints | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-35305-5_14\"},{\"title\":\"A novel rolling horizon product-centric MILP based approach for a real-world integrated production and distribution scheduling problem - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0305048325002270\"},{\"title\":\"A rolling horizon planning approach for short-term demand supply matching | Central European Journal of Operations Research | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10100-023-00891-z\"},{\"title\":\"A ROLLING-HORIZON APPROACH FOR MULTI-PERIOD OPTIMIZATION\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/7809.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Production scheduling in steel rolling mills with demand substitution: Rolling horizon implementation and approximations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0925527310001489\"},{\"title\":\"Learning-Guided Rolling Horizon Optimization for Long-Horizon Flexible Job-Shop Scheduling\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2502.15791v1\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query. Here are the search results:\n\n## Summary of Key Findings\n\nThe search returned 10 relevant research papers and articles covering the intersection of time limits, MIP gaps, rolling horizon approaches, and industrial scheduling/production planning:\n\n**Key Result: MIP Gaps and Time Limits in Rolling Horizon Scheduling**\n\nA recent study solved mathematical models using Cplex with a fixed time limit of one hour, achieving an average MIP gap of 0.30% for a deterministic model and 0.98% for a stochastic model.\n\n**Rolling Horizon Approaches in Production Planning**\n\nSolving complex stochastic production and scheduling problems in a rolling horizon scheme is a promising strategy to efficiently cover continuously occurring changes along the planning horizon, allowing production schedules to be revised regularly to adapt to new customer orders.\n\nThe rolling horizon method has been proposed to address integrated production planning and scheduling optimization, and since it can generally result in small-scale optimization models and fast solutions, it has been used in realistic industrial planning and scheduling problems.\n\n**MIP Gap Settings**\n\nA relative MIP gap of 15% has been used for subproblems resulting from decomposition of MILPs in rolling horizon approaches for demand-supply matching.\n\n**Time-Limited Optimization Trade-offs**\n\nResearch has shown that when time-limit is greater than 5 seconds, the GAP does not decrease considerably, suggesting diminishing returns beyond certain time thresholds in some scheduling problems.\n\nThe search results indicate that rolling horizon approaches with time-limited MIP solvers are a well-established technique in industrial production planning and scheduling, balancing computational tractability with solution quality.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8cb86c0c-b4e0-45a2-88f7-d218662adc3f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:53:30.926Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6e34d2c4-cad4-4720-89ed-4e6764b8e494","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"83cb15dd-0b4e-42bc-a934-6943f484a2a6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a0ac44e2d03f4edb2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MnYqxqjoBLbPyDhT7q5896","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT large neighborhood search LNS feasibility_pump portfolio worker incumbent improvement\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Large Neighborhood Search - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/09_lns.html\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"Adaptive Large Neighborhood Search (ALNS)\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/adaptive-large-neighborhood-search-alns-algorithm\"},{\"title\":\"7.7. Large neighborhood search (LNS): the Job-Shop Problem — or-tools User's Manual\",\"url\":\"https://acrogenesis.com/or-tools/documentation/user_manual/manual/metaheuristics/jobshop_lns.html\"},{\"title\":\"Large Neighborhood Search (LNS)\",\"url\":\"https://www.hexaly.com/algorithms/large-neighborhood-search-lns\"},{\"title\":\"Adaptive large neighborhood search for mixed integer programming | Mathematical Programming Computation | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12532-021-00209-7\"},{\"title\":\"Adaptive large-neighbourhood search for optimisation in answer-set programming - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0004370224001668\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"A General Large Neighborhood Search Framework for ...\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2020/file/e769e03a9d329b2e864b4bf4ff54ff39-Paper.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Large Neighborhood Search | Choco-solver\",\"url\":\"https://choco-solver.org/docs/solving/lns/\"}]\n\nI've successfully retrieved search results for your query about CP-SAT Large Neighborhood Search (LNS) and related components. Here's a summary of the findings:\n\n## CP-SAT's LNS Portfolio and Worker Strategy\n\nCP-SAT schedules its LNS strategies using a simple round-robin method, with worker threads executing iterations with the next strategy in the list when they finish their work, leading to a fair distribution of calls.\n\n## Incomplete Subsolvers\n\nCP-SAT uses 8 incomplete subsolvers including: feasibility_pump, graph_arc_lns, graph_cst_lns, graph_dec_lns, graph_var_lns, rins/rens, rnd_cst_lns, and rnd_var_lns.\n\nNotable incomplete solver strategies are large neighborhood search (LNS) and feasibility pumps, where the first tries to find a better solution by changing only a few variables, and the second tries to make infeasible/incomplete solutions feasible.\n\n## Portfolio Worker Configuration\n\nWith two or more workers, CP-SAT starts using incomplete subsolvers, i.e., heuristics such as LNS.\n\n## Incumbent Improvement\n\nIn practical examples, the 'routing_path_lns' strategy was the most successful, improving the incumbent solution 41 times out of 65 calls.\n\n## LNS Implementation Details\n\nIn CP-SAT's LNS, a copy of the model is made with a solution selected from the pool, a subset of variables is removed from the solution, the neighborhood of these variables is explored for a better solution, and the remaining variables are fixed to their current values.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"db9066c0-95a0-4a83-8a7e-0217502cdd88","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:53:31.387Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"83cb15dd-0b4e-42bc-a934-6943f484a2a6","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"62593704-43c9-4e4b-a301-d8ba5f32de39","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"a0ac44e2d03f4edb2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JuMkfi1jR1KckQGRnXYSVg","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Gurobi ImproveStartGap ImproveStartTime two phase MIP solve strategy switch\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Change solution strategy to standard (ImproveStartGap,ImproveStartNodes,ImproveStartTime) – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/5588450672529-Change-solution-strategy-to-standard-ImproveStartGap-ImproveStartNodes-ImproveStartTime\"},{\"title\":\"Parameter Guidelines - Gurobi Optimizer Reference Manual\",\"url\":\"https://docs.gurobi.com/projects/optimizer/en/current/concepts/parameters/guidelines.html\"},{\"title\":\"MIP Models - Gurobi Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.gurobi.com/documentation/9.5/refman/mip_models.html\"},{\"title\":\"Class GurobiSolverConfiguration | OPTANO.Modeling\",\"url\":\"https://docs.optano.net/modeling/current/api/OPTANO.Modeling.Optimization.Solver.Gurobi605.GurobiSolverConfiguration.html\"},{\"title\":\"GUROBI Options — AMPL Resources\",\"url\":\"https://dev.ampl.com/solvers/gurobi/options.html\"},{\"title\":\"Parameter Reference - Gurobi Optimizer Reference Manual\",\"url\":\"https://docs.gurobi.com/projects/optimizer/en/current/reference/parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"How to adjust the parameters of Gurobi in the process of solving？\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/yalmip/c/1A4QgfUaouc\"},{\"title\":\"How do I use MIP starts? – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/articles/360043834831-How-do-I-use-MIP-starts\"},{\"title\":\"Change solution strategy to standard (ImproveStartGap ...\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/5588450672529-Change-solution-strategy-to-standard-ImproveStartGap-ImproveStartNodes-ImproveStartTime-\"},{\"title\":\"Providing the optimal solution as warm start does not improve runtime of MIP. – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/22369950175889-Providing-the-optimal-solution-as-warm-start-does-not-improve-runtime-of-MIP\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Gurobi's ImproveStartGap, ImproveStartTime, and two-phase MIP solve strategy:\n\n## Overview\n\nThe ImproveStartTime and ImproveStartGap parameters can be used to modify your high-level solution strategy by allowing you to give up on proving optimality at a certain point in the search, and instead focus all attention on finding better feasible solutions from that point onward.\n\n## How the Parameters Work\n\nThe ImproveStartTime parameter allows you to make this transition after the specified time has elapsed, while the ImproveStartGap parameter makes the transition when the specified optimality gap has been achieved.\n\n## Two-Phase Strategy\n\nThese parameters allow to focus on improving the bound for some time in the beginning and then switch to another strategy. For example, with the setting ImproveStartTime = 500, the Gurobi Optimizer starts with the default setting and after 500 seconds, it then just focuses on improving the incumbent.\n\n## Strategy Switch Behavior\n\nThe parameters ImproveStartGap, ImproveStartNodes, and ImproveStartTime trigger solution improvement strategies where the Gurobi Optimizer only focuses on improving the incumbent to find a better feasible solution and essentially gives up on improving the best bound.\n\n## Customizing the Strategy\n\nIf you want to use a different strategy to switch to after the Improve parameters' conditions are met, you can start the optimization with setting MIPFocus=3 and check the gap, node count, and/or time in the MIP callback, then terminate the optimization in the callback as soon as reaching the specified limits, reset the parameters to their default values, and continue the optimization.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"c24cf740-6e37-44bf-b900-ab5d9e7ff603","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:53:32.378Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"62593704-43c9-4e4b-a301-d8ba5f32de39","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
